  (485110565069::bigint, 485129510123::bigint, null::bigint, 413191686365::bigint, '05/03 CALL NOTES


1. Legacy Systems &amp; Operational Debt
Challenge/Pain Point: Outdated, unsupported systems; operational debt; difficulty modernizing.
Need: Expertise in extracting, virtualizing, and making use of data from legacy systems without full-scale replacement.
2. Disparate &amp; Unjoined Data Sets
Challenge/Pain Point: Data is siloed, hard to access, and not integrated across brands or functions.
Need: Solutions for data virtualization, integration, and building accessible data platforms for analytics and decision-making.
3. Underdeveloped Loyalty Programs
Challenge/Pain Point: Loyalty programs not as advanced as competitors; uncertainty about unifying programs across brands.
Need: Strategic advice and technical solutions for building differentiated, effective loyalty programs tailored to each brand.
4. Retail Media Opportunity
Challenge/Pain Point: Recognize retail media as a high-margin, strategic opportunity but lack in-house capability and strategy.
Need: Guidance and practical help in setting up retail media propositions, leveraging data for monetization, and building business cases.
5. Cloud Migration &amp; SaaS Transition
Challenge/Pain Point: Ongoing move to Google Cloud and SaaS; risk of inefficiency or cost overruns.
Need: Pragmatic, experienced support to ensure cloud migration and SaaS adoption are efficient and add value.
6. AI &amp; Innovation
Challenge/Pain Point: Uncertainty about which AI use cases are valuable; desire to experiment and learn quickly.
Need: Hands-on, rapid prototyping and proof-of-concept work to identify and demonstrate valuable AI applications (as GT did for Boots).
7. Finance Systems Modernization
Challenge/Pain Point: Need to modernize finance systems; considering Oracle but aware it’s not a complete solution.
Need: Help in standing up data solutions that provide predictive and actionable insights for finance teams, even before full system replacement.
8. Cultural &amp; Organizational Change
Challenge/Pain Point: Need to shift from a partner-focused, traditional culture to a more agile, data-driven, and innovative mindset.
Need: Collaborative, partnership-based approach to change management and capability building, rather than a top-down vendor model.
9. Resource Constraints &amp; Market Headwinds
Challenge/Pain Point: Limited investment capacity; need to balance short-term efficiency with long-term transformation.
Need: Cost-effective, high-impact interventions that deliver quick wins and build momentum for larger change.
How GT Can Help:
Rapid prototyping and proof-of-concept development (as demonstrated with Boots).
Data integration and virtualization expertise.
Retail media strategy and implementation support.
Loyalty program design and technical enablement.
Collaborative, partnership-based working style—augmenting John Lewis teams rather than replacing them.
Practical, business-focused AI and analytics solutions.
Guidance on cloud migration and SaaS adoption.
Change management and capability building.
**Summary:**
John Lewis needs practical, business-focused, and collaborative support to modernize its technology, unlock value from data, advance loyalty and retail media, and experiment with AI—without the risk and cost of large, slow, traditional IT projects. GT’s experience and approach align well with these needs.', 'VS', '2026-04-14 10:20'),
  (482755707066::bigint, 16199822573::bigint, null::bigint, 13348058097::bigint, 'Presentation that we shown with the client on a call 02/04', 'VS', '2026-04-03 03:12'),
  (459437032676::bigint, 474457486550::bigint, null::bigint, 403086050494::bigint, 'Discussion re ERP advice - Gorgeous Products - February 20, 2026View on Bluedot


General notes

OverviewLuke Frey (operations lead) met with GT''s John Harris (CTO/co-founder), Nikita Gorskykh (CEO/co-founder), and Victoria (sales ops) alongside Mark (CEO) and Ruth Salsbury (CFO) to discuss technology and data reporting challenges. The company is an eCommerce hair and beauty retailer doing £30M last year and targeting £35M+ this year, selling 6,000 simple products plus 3,000 virtual bundle SKUs across 14 channels including two websites, multiple marketplaces (Debenhams, Superdrug, The Range), and Amazon. Key pain points include siloed Amazon data, poor Magento reporting, manual spreadsheet-based financial consolidation, stock cost valuation issues (FIFO/average costing), three-way invoice matching, and weekly supplier sales reports requiring significant manual effort. GT proposed a phased approach — Phase 1: BA and architecture review (single-figure thousands); Phase 2: process improvement and system design; Phase 3: build. Budget expectation is £50,000–£100,000. NDA to be exchanged. Luke''s team is also evaluating Acumatica ERP.


Action ItemsLuke Frey:
Send updated system architecture diagram to GT team (current diagram exists but is approximately 2 years old and needs review/updating)
Document current reporting processes and reports used weekly/monthly to prepare for BA engagement
Await GT''s proposal from Victoria before proceeding with Phase 1
Schedule intro call with Acumatica ERP (noted they have out-of-the-box Magento and Amazon integrations; call not yet formally booked)
John Harris / GT Team:
Send NDA to Luke Frey for signature (or accept Luke''s NDA — whichever is quicker)
John Harris and Nikita Gorskykh to discuss offline the appropriate Phase 1 team composition (BA for approximately one week, plus data executive or architect for a couple of days)
Victoria to prepare a phased proposal including ballpark estimates for all phases, not just Phase 1, with cost and time breakdowns
GT to define and present a compelling alternative to traditional ERP platforms


TopicsCompany Background and Business Overview (2:01)
Luke Frey provided an overview of the business — an eCommerce retailer in the hair and beauty sector. The company holds approximately 6,000 simple product SKUs and an additional 3,000 virtual bundle SKUs, where bundles are never physically packed together but fulfilled as individual component SKUs. The business sells across 14 channels comprising two owned websites, multiple third-party marketplaces including Debenhams, Superdrug, and The Range, plus Amazon. Turnover was £30M last year and is projected to exceed £35M this year. The company is privately owned with a significant portion of the management team planning to exit within the next three to five years, which influences the appetite for large capital investment. The business described its operational fulfilment as working well but identified data consolidation and financial reporting as critical weaknesses.
ERP Evaluation History and Limitations (3:13)
The company has previously evaluated SAP and NetSuite and received a quote from NetSuite of approximately £100,000 upfront integration cost plus approximately £60,000 per year ongoing — a figure Luke described as causing him to ''fall off his chair.'' John Harris explained that SAP and NetSuite follow an end-to-end model that assumes businesses will fit entirely to their processes and deploy all modules. This model is not well-suited to businesses with an existing functioning WMS, as ERP vendors want to replace rather than integrate. John noted that this is often overkill even for enterprises, not just SMEs. The team agreed that a full ERP deployment is unlikely to be necessary and that the core problem is a data and reporting challenge rather than an operational or fulfilment one. The company is also exploring Acumatica, which was noted to have out-of-the-box Magento and Amazon integrations, though no formal conversation has taken place yet.
Current Technology Stack and Integration Architecture (3:43)
The business uses Magento as its primary eCommerce platform, which also acts as the consolidation point for all marketplace channels except Amazon. Amazon operates as a completely siloed system with only a direct integration to the WMS for order processing — no financial or reporting connection exists between Amazon and the rest of the business. The WMS is operated by a third-party 3PL provider and is not owned or controlled by the company, limiting the ability to modify or replace it. An older system called Orderwise was previously used and functioned closer to an ERP, providing financial reporting, but the business is in the process of removing it. A systems architecture diagram exists but is approximately two years out of date. Ruth and Luke noted that there is also a separate stock management system distinct from the WMS itself. Magento was described as very poor at producing financial or channel-specific reporting.
Key Financial and Reporting Pain Points (13:08)
Ruth Salsbury outlined several critical reporting challenges. Amazon data must be manually extracted from multiple sources within the Amazon platform before being combined. Stock cost valuations are inaccurate and time-consuming to verify each month, with the business currently using a single static cost price rather than a FIFO or weighted average method. This directly impacts margin calculations across all 14 channels. Channel-by-channel departmental reporting is done entirely via spreadsheets. Weekly supplier sales reports must be manually collated and reformatted for each supplier — a task that consumes one staff member''s entire Monday morning every week. Ruth described the ideal outcome as a process where reports are auto-generated and staff simply perform a final check before distribution. Ruth also flagged three-way invoice matching as an important but currently unresolved process requirement.
Proposed Solution Approach — Phased and Data-Centric (16:05)
John Harris proposed a phased engagement model inspired by the ETL (Extract, Transform, Load) methodology used at scale during his work with Nectar Card, where data from multiple partners including Sainsbury''s, BP, and Homebase was extracted, transformed, and loaded into a single reporting environment over a weekend cycle. For this client, John suggested that rather than deploying a full ERP, GT could investigate automating data extraction from all existing sources, transforming it into a unified data warehouse or data lake, and building automated reporting on top. Nikita suggested a ''low hanging fruit'' approach — using a BA to identify where maximum gains can be achieved with minimum investment, potentially supplemented by a data scientist or data engineer. The team agreed it is likely a data and automation challenge rather than an ERP requirement. Nikita noted that a proper BI reporting layer alone might resolve the majority of issues.
Business Process Simplification (19:19)
Ruth raised an important point that many existing processes may be unnecessarily complex due to the limitations of current systems, meaning that automating current processes may not be the optimal outcome. She described a situation where completing one task requires a specific sequence of other tasks — going from A to B to C to M to D rather than directly from A to D. John agreed, stating the key question is not just how to automate what exists today, but how to identify the smartest and simplest way to get the required data. He noted that automating 25 steps when 2 would suffice is counterproductive. This framing reinforced the need for proper BA engagement upfront to review and potentially redesign processes before any automation is built.
WMS Replacement Consideration (20:39)
Nikita asked whether the WMS itself was a candidate for replacement, noting it is typically the heaviest part of any system overhaul. Ruth and Luke gave slightly differing views — Ruth acknowledged the current stock management system is better than previous solutions but not perfect, and that she is open to improvements as part of a holistic solution. Luke clarified that the WMS is owned and operated by the third-party 3PL provider, meaning the company may not have the ability to replace it even if desired. Luke emphasised that operationally the fulfilment process works well — orders are processed and dispatched efficiently — and the WMS should be left in place. John confirmed that leaving the WMS as-is and treating this purely as a reporting and data challenge is the right framing given the client''s goals and constraints.
NDA and Proposal Next Steps (25:04)
John Harris confirmed that an NDA should be signed between GT and the client before deeper information sharing occurs. He offered to send GT''s NDA or to sign the client''s version, with Luke agreeing GT should send theirs. Victoria (GT sales ops) was tasked with preparing a formal phased proposal. Luke confirmed he would update and share the existing systems architecture diagram and begin documenting current reporting processes and procedures in preparation for the BA engagement. Both parties agreed to proceed collaboratively and review the proposal with Mark and Ruth before committing to Phase 1.
Phased Project Structure and Estimated Costs (25:55)
John Harris outlined a three-phase project structure. Phase 1 would be a rapid discovery and validation exercise — approximately one week of BA time and two days of architect or data executive time — designed as a ''fail fast'' step to validate assumptions. Cost would be in the single-figure thousands. If assumptions prove incorrect, work stops and the client returns to ERP vendors. Phase 2 would cover business process improvement recommendations and architectural design of interfaces and ETL pipelines, with an estimated duration of two to ten weeks depending on complexity. Phase 3 would involve building the agreed integrations and automated reports, with pricing scaling accordingly. Luke requested that ballpark estimates for all phases be included in the initial proposal so the business can make an informed decision before committing to Phase 1. The budget expectation from Luke is in the range of £50,000–£100,000 total, which John confirmed is realistic and achievable under the phased model.
Stock Costing and FIFO Valuation Issues (27:37)
Luke and Ruth discussed in detail the cost pricing challenge. The business purchases stock at varying prices throughout the year, but the WMS stores only a single cost price per SKU. This means a price increase from a supplier immediately inflates the cost price of all existing stock for that brand, even stock purchased a year earlier at a lower price. The ideal solution is a FIFO (first in, first out) or weighted average cost per unit recalculated dynamically based on current stock holdings. John Harris illustrated the downstream impact — a product currently showing a 20% margin on a marketplace could drop to 2% margin once existing cheaper stock is exhausted, and the business currently lacks visibility into this forecasting risk. Accurate cost pricing underpins all margin calculations and marketplace pricing strategies.
Scope Management and Risk of Scope Creep (31:02)
John Harris warned explicitly about the risk of scope creep once technical teams are engaged, noting that each new discovery tends to open additional areas for improvement, which can inadvertently replicate the scale and cost of a full ERP implementation. He recommended that the client clearly define which problems will be addressed in each phase and which will be deferred. Luke acknowledged that even during the call his mind was moving toward additional improvements. John advised that a COO-level function within the business would need to manage internal expectations and communicate clearly which items are in scope versus deferred. The analogy used was ''pulling on a thread'' — tidying one part risks unravelling the whole ball of string.
Long-Term Staffing and Ongoing Support Options (36:26)
John Harris suggested that after initial improvements are in place, the client might find value in embedding a dedicated data analyst with technical skills into Ruth''s team, either hired directly onto the client''s payroll or retained through GT on a monthly service fee. He noted that sourcing such a resource from Ukraine or Poland would offer strong English language skills, relevant technical expertise, and a materially lower cost than an equivalent UK-based hire. This was presented not as a cost-cutting pitch but as a practical option to sustain ongoing reporting improvements. John referenced Boots as an existing GT client operating under a similar ongoing engagement model, where GT is used to solve higher-level data problems and is presented with new challenges over time.
Bespoke System Concerns and Vendor Lock-In Risk (42:43)
Luke raised concerns — primarily on behalf of Mark and Ruth — about the risks associated with bespoke systems, including dependency on a single developer and the risk of being locked in if that person or company becomes unavailable. John acknowledged this is a valid concern and stated that GT''s approach includes thorough documentation to ensure transferability — any solution delivered would be understandable and maintainable by another party. He drew a parallel to the current situation where institutional knowledge is locked in Ruth''s team and Excel spreadsheets, which carries equivalent or greater risk. Nikita added that GT operates a governance policy requiring adequate documentation as standard, and that working with a company rather than an individual mitigates single-point-of-failure risk, as GT maintains a team of engineers who can pick up any project. GT also highlighted their interest in long-term client relationships as an additional safeguard.', 'VS', '2026-02-20 08:51'),
  (454499500222::bigint, 443118107877::bigint, null::bigint, null::bigint, 'Proposal attached', 'VS', '2026-02-15 12:31'),
  (437585845497::bigint, 453943531716::bigint, null::bigint, 278186195187::bigint, '29/01 Andrew Muzzele shared 


Hi Jacqui &amp; Paul


It was great to talk openly this afternoon and here is a summary of my thoughts.


As per the analogy with the house around foundational issues, extensions and redecoration - the key gaps I think you need to close to enable solid delivery with the maximum efficiency are outlined below, together with the solution and recommended approach.


Please don''t share this as it is due to sensitive comments below. These comments should be removed before sharing with the team.


Let me know what you think, Andrew


THE GAPS


Foundations
This breakdowns into two areas: Tech &amp; Process
• Tech - Areas of code to be addressed to optimise the code and minimise the time/costs for future changes, reduce maintenance effort and bugs.
• Insight Layer - Enable better more flexible reporting and facilitate easier testing 
• Gateway / Assembly - To ease setup and install of the gateways and reduce the spec of boxes required 
• The Rest - From talking to the team the rest is pretty solid but could do with tidying up when it next needs to be touched for new features


• Process - There are number of areas that should be addressed to improve the efficiency of delivery and the quality of your solution
Architecture - You need a handful of diagrams to outline the current architecture and target architecture so everyone is aligned with the vision and understands how your product works from a technical perspective. This reduces your dependance on one or two people that understand how things work and helps development and testing.Automated Testing Coverage - Improve your coverage of automated tests to enable earlier and quicker testing by developers and reduce the number of bugs / delivery timelines.Requirements / Non-functional Requirements - An outline of the product and how it works from a users perspective to aid onboarding, testing and developmentNon-functional testing - Ensure your non-functional testing is up to date. I don''t believe your security testing has been done since Jan 2024 and should be done regularly with the current levels of change and I don''t know if you have done Business Continuity / Disaster Recovery plans or testing.Agile Improvements - More accurately load sprints and track burn down rates to improve estimates going forwards. Also increase the quality of and attendance of demosCommunications - Comms from the tech team to the rest of the business need to be lifted up a level providing concise summaries with clear updates on progressClient Engagement - Consider doing pilots with one or more clients - listen to what they need and deliver on the basis that they will be happy to do PR upon successful completion / buy X UnitsExtensions
New features as per the Roadmap in the GT deck and Andre''s doc. Andre''s estimates look good and the context / detail is helpful. I think it needs a clearer timeline, confirmation from the business with respect to the changes vs the GT deck - eg: B2C comes before ICS but otherwise content is good.


Redecoration
Everything from the install to the websites, apps and reports could be more usable and the look &amp; feel can be improved to engage clients.


THE SOLUTIONS


Foundations
Tech &amp; Architecture - Get an Architect to review architecture and code. This could be positioned to support Tim and document his vision for his and everyone''s benefit. This will also give direction on whether we are doing the right thing in persisting with the existing code base.Test Coverage - Two automation testers brought in to increase coverage to an acceptable level in a single burst while the rest of the team focus on new featuresReq / NFRs - Get Andre document (if it is not already done in a wiki that I haven''t seen)NFRs - Get Ben / Tim to book / execute pen / BC/DR testing when it is appropriate.Overall / Agile Improvements / Communications - Get CTO / Andrew in part-time to get right processes and comms in place and oversee timely deliveryClient Engagement - Get Andre / Steve to select ASC clients with needs that closely match our roadmap for input to development and a case study. BTW if they are not aligned to our roadmap something may not be right with our roadmap.Extension
Bring in addition resources to deliver the outlined roadmap within the timelines and determine the capabilities of the current team


Redecoration
Improve UX / UI


THE RECOMMENDED APPROACH


GT provides the following resources to work with the current team for at least 6 months to enable the Tech team to address the historic tech issues, deliver new features against the roadmap, and communicate clearly with the rest of the business and clients.
0.4 x CTO for 6 months - Andrew to work with MySense 2 days pw initially to get initiatives outline in place and then review input with a view to moving to 1 day pw0.5 - 1 x Architect for 1 month - Document current / future architecture, review code and confirm direction on whether we should start again on code base / replace team and then review architectural effort1 x UX/UI for 1 month - Refresh the client facing website and mobile app with input from Shona and others. Part-time after the first 1 month.2 x Data Engineer / Analyst for 6 months - To re-write the Insight Layer (potentially based on Annie''s concepts) after this you are likely to need to retain at least one of these resources if people leave from the data team.2 x Backend or Fullstack Engineers for at least 6 months - To deliver new features as you have no full time backend engineers at this time and you are likely to need to retain at least one of these resources.1 x Automated testers for at least 6 months - To increase automated test coverage to an acceptable level in a single burst while the rest of the team focuses on new features.Notes
At the end of month 1, with input from the architect and the rest of the teamreview whether we should start again on the code base review whether to replace any teams / team members with GT resources / totally outsourceWith the exception of the first three roles the rest are aligned to Andre''s resourcing plan for 2026.', 'VS', '2026-01-30 09:52'),
