Introduction of JeffJeff  brings extensive experience in the sports industry, having worked with organizations such as NBC Universal, World Surf League, and Kelly Slater Wave Company. His background in sports content distribution, corporate finance, and business development has helped shape the vision for this project. Jeff is particularly passionate about leveraging technology to enhance the sports experience, and his involvement in the project has been crucial in bringing together a team of experts across various fields.

NeedsOptimization of Sales and Fan Engagement: Teams and stadiums struggle with sales processes, particularly smaller teams that lack the resources to manage sophisticated sales systems. There is a need to streamline ticket sales and fan engagement to drive revenue and improve experiences.
AI to Transform Sales: AI is seen as a tool to automate and enhance sales processes, making them more efficient, personalized, and data-driven. The goal is to improve not only ticket sales but also how teams interact with their fans, offering personalized services and creating stronger connections.
Data Quality and Integration: To effectively implement AI, there is a need for high-quality, organized data. The current data across teams and platforms like Ticketmaster and Salesforce may not be structured properly, which limits its utility. Teams need help optimizing their data to leverage AI for better results.
ChallengesData Ownership and Privacy: One of the main challenges discussed is managing personal fan data, especially with platforms like Ticketmaster that control much of this data. Ensuring data privacy and navigating the complexities of who owns and controls this data will be key.
Adoption of AI: While AI is a buzzword in the industry, there’s skepticism about whether it is necessary for all use cases. The challenge is to ensure that AI is applied where it can truly add value and not just for the sake of using cutting-edge technology.
Data Infrastructure: Many teams do not have the proper data infrastructure to support AI applications. Teams may have siloed data, unstructured information, or outdated systems that could hinder the adoption of AI if not addressed.

Next StepsConduct a Discovery Phase: The first step is to conduct a discovery phase, where the team will gather more data on current sales processes, identify pain points, and assess the readiness of teams’ data for AI implementation.
Prototype Development: After the discovery phase, the team will develop prototypes for AI-driven ticket sales and fan engagement tools. These will be tested with a small set of teams and stadiums to refine the approach.
Partnerships and Data Integration: The team will begin exploring potential partnerships with companies like Ticketmaster to understand how they can access the necessary data for AI applications while addressing privacy concerns. This will also involve testing API integrations and understanding data-sharing limitations.
Scale and Expansion: Once the solutions are validated, the team plans to scale the platform across multiple stadiums and teams, ultimately building a data-driven ecosystem that can be used to drive new revenue streams and improve fan engagement.
Team Formation: The team will focus on assembling the right partners and experts to support the project, ensuring that all aspects—tech, AI, data, and sports management—are covered by skilled professionals.

In the attachment there''s a screenshot from the presentation that Jeff showed to us on a call.', 'VS', '2025-02-27 11:41'),
