  (122838620367::bigint, 103818355949::bigint, null::bigint, 9638126063::bigint, '15/01 CALL NOTES:

Summary of the key points Chris touched on throughout the conversation:
Putting “WorkVibes” on Hold
Chris explains that “WorkVibes” (a culture/HR software project) must be paused.
The main reason is to focus on generating revenue first rather than juggling multiple new initiatives.
He envisions eventually “incubating” WorkVibes when they can “responsibly” do so—likely Q3 or Q4.
Focus on a Single “Puzzle” (The Momentum Factory)
Previously, Chris felt he was trying to build several “puzzles” (projects) at once (e.g., a podcast network, MSpark, etc.).
Now, he wants to focus on one core consulting model, referring to it as the “Momentum Factory” (formerly “Impact Factory”).
This model scales the work he and his team did for MSpark to other clients—offering hands-on, “doer” consulting services (e.g., change management, brand transformation).
Ideal Client Profile
Chris prefers partnering with larger clients (e.g., billion-dollar companies) that have clear C-suite buy-in.
He cites MSpark as a smaller-scale “turnaround” case; meanwhile, companies like LendingPoint or other high-revenue organizations are more desirable for future engagements.
Personal Priorities and Multiple Roles
Chris mentions his second book on sales, highlighting that he doesn’t want to be pigeonholed solely as a “culture and leadership” speaker.
He notes the practical reality of needing consistent income and that too many simultaneous projects jeopardize that stability.
Current &amp; Prospective Projects
MSpark: A challenging turnaround client with some internal friction; Chris and team have done extensive revamp work and hope to continue once leadership is aligned.
LendingPoint: Chris is working to secure a proposal, though success depends on whether the relevant executive (Raul) has the authority to make big changes.
Secretariat: Chris is exploring deeper involvement with this expert witness/audit organization, potentially bringing in development partners.
Commercial Real Estate (Sands Investment Group): Chris sees untapped potential for innovation in this space, especially around market analysis and tech-driven improvements.
Strategy Going Forward
Chris plans to nurture these engagements, leveraging strategic partners (like tech/development teams) as needed.
He will re-visit building out software solutions (like WorkVibes) only once the firm’s consulting arm and revenue streams are secure.
Overall, Chris’s main emphasis is on focused growth—building out the “Momentum Factory” approach and prioritizing revenue-driving partnerships before investing further energy into new products such as WorkVibes.', 'VS', '2025-01-15 10:23'),
