  (87158741197::bigint, 16160433381::bigint, null::bigint, 7667569885::bigint, 'John''s notes:

Hi folks - setting up this thread to prepare a proposal for Mercer. I have posted detailed notes in Hubspot, but we have an opportunity to place an architect to review the architecture of an existing .Net product and potentially to rebuild some of the front end and to provide some data visualisation of existing usage patterns.
6:03
John Harris
THe product is sold to HR departments at big enterprises, it has a whole series of data and reports relevant to HR - typical salaries country by country, role by role for benchmarking, infomration on different legislation in each country, niche information like travel allowances, tax rules etc etc. So its an enormous database that is then consumed online by the clients. Today it is basically sold as an “all in” package, but they want to look at what it woudl take to make it more granular and allow certain bundles of reports and data to be sold to customers who just want certain information and dont want to pay for everything.
6:05
John Harris
So we advised them that they would need to think like Apple around bundling together different product offerings - good, better and best. To help understand how those bundles should work we said we would take data that they have today showing which areas of the site users go to and when and we will then visualise that data into a mix panel in order to help them understand what those use patterns are and what should be bundled together. So that is engagement 1, a good data person with visualisaion and reporting skills who can take that data and make it more accesible in a mix panel
6:05
John Harris
THe second piece is an archiect to review the existing .Net back end architecture and understand what the current sitaution is, how scalable for the future etc etc.
6:06
John Harris
The third piece is giving them a cost to potentially rebuild some of the front end based on the wireframes we designed for them earlier this year.
6:07
John Harris
Finally and a bit of a nice to have, we want to give them an idea of what it would take to develop a simple working demo where we could show how their data could drive a LLM and allow clients to ask questions from a chat bot to get insigh from teh data - potentially this would be a great way of getting new clients to realise the power of the tool and for exsiging ones to see more of the wider data that they dont buy today
John HarrisJohn Harris  6:09 PM

So as a proposal, I think we need to do time and materials for 4-6 week engagemnt for a data visualisation person, 2-3 weeks of .Net architect and perhaps 8 weeks of front end developer. And maybe a 2 week rapid concept development on the LLM chatbot. They have budget left this year they would like to spend, I think realistically they might be able to spend £25k, perhaps £30k. Not sure if we can do eveyrthing for that price but would be greaet if we could get close.', 'VS', '2024-11-27 03:32'),
  (87053373643::bigint, 35974896876::bigint, null::bigint, 33230436563::bigint, '26/11 CALL NOTES (Raul, Jordan, John, Ivan, Andy, Vika)

Deliverables (mentioned by John):
Discovery Phase Outputs:
Detailed Assessment Report:
Organized into three sections: Good, Bad, and Ugly.
Identifies optimal areas, areas needing rapid action, and long-term improvement opportunities.
Diagrammatical Representations:
Updated system diagrams illustrating current architecture, flows, and interfaces.
Useful for internal teams, compliance, and investor confidence.
Prioritized Action List:
Outlines immediate fixes and longer-term priorities based on assessment findings.
Architecture Review:
Analysis of the current architecture, including:
Codebase, especially .NET components.
Third-party add-ons and APIs.
Salesforce backend, focusing on performance issues like latency.
Assessment of efficiency, risk, and compatibility with current and future needs.
Operational Procedures and Cybersecurity:
Operational Procedures:
Review and recommendations for ITIL-aligned processes, focusing on:
Incident management.
Problem management.
Change management.
Cybersecurity Posture:
Evaluation of privileged access policies, two-factor authentication, and other critical security measures.
Suggestions for quick fixes and long-term improvements.
Documentation:
Mid-to-high-level documentation of findings and architecture.
Provides valuable, actionable insights for technical teams and stakeholders.
Timelines and Milestones:
Timelines refined during the project, with ongoing updates about progress and findings.
Deliverables provided incrementally, beginning as soon as the discovery phase starts.
Business Value:
Deliverables designed to have direct business value:
Supporting compliance and security.
Improving system performance and operational efficiency.
Enhancing decision-making for future system development.

Governance and Collaboration
Weekly or bi-weekly steering board meetings for project oversight and decision-making.
Separate technical meetings managed by Ivan for deeper discussions.
Collaboration with the client''s CTO and other technical staff to ensure alignment.', 'VS', '2024-11-26 11:05'),
  (83861056761::bigint, 16160433381::bigint, null::bigint, 7667569885::bigint, 'Mercer - TAAP platform
Sam - based in New Jersey, brought up in MOntreal, heads up product in Mikes space for a number of different products, 
Smaller product group than other areas but v fast growing....
Quite an agile group, strong processes, great team to implement
Not always had the vision of what we want to do from a platform and commercial perspective,
Been quite tactical deliverer of product content...
Tries to take a small startup perspective.... 
Yana - from Ukraine, based in Poland in our Warsaw office, is account manager for the existing clients and responsible for developing new markets...
Mike - working closelyw ith Sam, trying to drive commercial proposition and strategy, how do we scale, in Europe grown by 50% this year, trebled in side three years, but we think we can do an awful lot more but  
Positioned us as experts in data and life science...
Sam joined through an acqusition in 2010 so is used to a smaller organisation....
Andy - what is driving that growth?
Mike - the platform has tremendous value to clients, contains data around historical salary increases, allows you to plan accordingly
Hot tpics - so data currently around the EU pay review directive
Route to market is primarily through our existing Mercer clients who are already buying data from us..
Its an unlimited data model, annual subscription, clients see great value from it. 
So growth all through an existing cluent base. And still not getting anywhere near them all. 
Hard to describe WHAT it is, there is a whole marketing branding play here wehre we want thee clitnt to get the value quicker. 
At the minute it relies on a human bringing the platform to them
But we want to get a Freemium model where they can get just enough data to see vlaue and then decide to get subscroption. 
TAAP plus and TAAP enterprise
But doesnt really distinguish or disaggregate or show closed data sets...
Its all bundled with an "all in" in mind....
So we didnt need to geek things or break it down.... 
Some parts of it can be brokedn down and you get one report, discrete data sets on the Data tab. 
3.5k for Compensation planning tab for example. 
But the user of that could be shown some other pieces to excite them...
And on the Insights page, can now break some of it down into individual reports, they have already down the work to make the data suitably discrete at the back end....
They do show some data on fremium, but this changes month by month on what they decide to share etc, goal is to get a teaser out there....
And the other thing is that they want to think about having  a reusable architecture and infrastructure that c ould be rinsed and repeasted for other business units withother types of data......
This is built on .Net , built on this because its free open source, smaller group that has now become bigger.
Pressure on tech teams to come off that architecture but Sam nervous about it
Each of these reports is effectively sitting in a content management system behind the scenes, so each item has the tags that its authorised to show on this page....
JH - outlined the work for Boots on chatbot, and suggested that you could use an LLM to then be able to scan all the data in TAAP and then produce an output of content that coul dbe interesting etc etc. And use that as the loss leader etc....
Andy - has anyone analysed teh unit profitability on each of these data areas?
Yes, 86% profit margi across the board...
So any small incremental lift is printing money
