  (19956217020::bigint, 8039860952::bigint, null::bigint, null::bigint, 'Excellent discussions last week wtih Ade and their head of data science, ANdy Morris. 
V detailed notes below. Short version is John to go back with some ideas on areas we could help - action by end of this week. Ade and Andy will potentially present us with a business challenge at some point where we will be asked to bring our innovative and nimble capabilities to solve a business challenge that their current Indian provider TCS would struggle with. 

Andy Morris - head of analytics, has been there for 2 years.
Ade, I dont mix up advisory with business.....  but this is unique...  FIrst time in 10 years I have done a recommendation like this...
Loved the VMWare session, the dialogue was not just about VMWare, it was to do with change, culture, behaviour.... 
Andy Morris - Aid and I had a chat the other day, decided that one of the ways to seed all the conversation to get it to how we could come back and help is to undersatnd a bit about the orgnaisation.
For the sake of simplicity probably best to talk about Boots, tehy are hte major international bit of Walgreen Boots alliance.
9 quarters of market share growth against a backdrop of really tough evironemnntm so brillianit retail perfornance,
Boots has one of the oldest loyalty schemes in. UK, Advantage card, 16 million active members, for beauty and health probably have a greater level of depth than club card.
So the amount of data available is incredible, our competitors would kill for it. We are pretty good at marketing execution,
My role covers quite a broad range of stuff, from market customer insight to anlaytics data science, manahement information through to technoology platofrm and data engineering. technology wise, we are like most large organisation have a large mix of legacy or heritage and then strategic, azure based data lake house, moving towards more of a lakehouse architecture, started that 4 years ago, MS and what they offer is changing radically so part of the challenge in platform space is how do I simplify waht we have got and reduce run cost so i can deliver more for our business in terms of analytics and market execution for smae cost. Databricks in the mix, looking at how we can reduce the cost of the technolgoy platform, remove some of othe more expensive Azure components.
On the pharmacy side of things, interesting areas in the organisation, up utnil about now, all the data we hold about our patients, (we think pateints very separately to customers though its effectively same hiuman being), so now holidng in the lakehouse custome and pateint date in teh same place for first itme ever.... So really intersting opportunities, this is massively exciting, we can find new ways to grow our business but not yet sure what they are... But properly understanding the human being, their needs, etc will deliver fantastic outcomes but not quite sure what they are yet, but gut feel is it could be huge.
Came in as interim for all things digital 2 years ago, then most of the rest of it as interim CDO then joined permanent on 1st August, so also undersatnd the model of transtiioning talent from a consutlancy kind of thing, its an interesting model and for the right people and right roles its beneficial to both sides of the equation.
Our pharmacy business has been losing market shareon prescriptions probably month on mnoth for the last 10 years, its a slow and steady decline, pharmacy is very low margin business, w hich is why folks like Llloyds are going bust, why there are fewer pharmacies in Sainsburys.... 
Losing market share to independents and some online...
Online not as big as you would think as a competitor btw...
Boots shutting 300 stores in the next years, but all those stores are so close to another boots store taht 99.9 percent of the UK population are within 15 minutes of a bOots store, the number after 300 moves to 98.8% so not that significantin a change. Al lthe folks displaced will be offered a role in the next nearerst boots store, so its rationalisation rather than redundancy.... 
DIstribution of revenue online v phsuicaly about 20/80. Online grew in covid. Boots woke up to the online pieceduring covid, had had a very old. unloved site, so technology wise got a shot in the arm at that point.... 
3.5 yeras ago werent funding their app, thats all changed now though post covid, app is important in the digital world. 
Opportunities for our business, government have announced about 650million for how to get pharmacies to take more work from GPs..... If we have got say 50% market share, 300m of govt money should be coming our way to develop those services, blood testing, blood pressure checks etc...
Probably as a country moving to more of an Ireland model where less healthcare is free... but not full private US style....
Brand trust in Boots is higher than brand trust in the NHS!!
Current data team - in the execution against data space, we have probably 6 peopel in the organisation (55,000 people in total org including retail), 6 are the data scientists, working in a roving capacity, a couple of them are specislisats in space range and location. One of the things we do quite well, is that space range and location capability, very solid. I need to improve how I prioritise what the remaining genuine data science capabilit yis working on to make sure its the biggest most valuable work. A lot of work int he marekting execution mainly as working over so many platforms. ANd sometimes its adapting to the changes from marketring, so 30 people supporting the activity related to marketing. This financial year which closes today, we will have sent just short of 1.5 billion emails. 
Andy B - where are the BAs, how do they interepret data to bring it to the righ feeds etc? Often thats where things break down in these models , the time lag between data and actionable insight.
And how savvy are your marketing team?
Andy Baynes
11:49 AM
How many years of historical data?
What condition is the data in?
Which division/department do the analysts belong to and what tools are they using?
How is data currently being used for customer engagement?
How is data currently being used for sales growth?
What are Boots’ commercial or strategic priorities for 2024
Is there a competitor that provides a model of best practice on how data is supporting their growth strategy?
how much is marketing really using data from loyalty scheme?
What are the business goals, ie is there a desire to grow the online business both retail and pharmacy?
Is there a goal and desire to improve the user experience? App versus email etc? 
What about GDPR for patient data?
IS there a scope to identify patients and consumers where upsell of diagnostic testing is appropriate
is the loyalty data driving targeted campaigns, if so is it store driven, or online?
Do you do big data basket level analytics to then determine shelf space decision making? 
COst optimisation for Azure - when did you last have a data soltuion architect review the set up and suggest optimisations and cost down measures? Could we look at the architectures and determine infra optimisations, VMWare, IBM storage etc....
Would there be niche products, such as testing and diagnostics that you would want to target more effectively with data?
What skillsets are currently deployed offshore, waht tasks are they being given to do?
Business goals
- What are Boots’ commercial or strategic priorities for 2024
- Is there a stated business desire to grow the online business both retail and pharmacy?
retail - 9 quarters of growth, largely driven in beauty space, had a very succesful launch of a new number 7 product in skin care, which has helped underpin that growth and ensure it continues. So plan is for gross profit growth in retail space on a year on year basis but a largely flat net profit as some of the headwinds are tough, ie increase in national living wage, expecing a 9% increase again next year, 50k employees at that level. Boots had done well at energy hedging but those hedges now run out so they are going to hit, so overall hold at net profit flat.
Focus on core areas of skin care, fragrance, cosmetics, one of the things we are trying to support that growth is a beauty only store, there is a shop being prepared on that basis in Battersea Power station, opens end of year to see what a beauty only proposition with high end feel has..... All sort of things aroudn how toimprove gross profit, ranging in stores etc...
pharmacy some basics to be fixed in arrasting the decline in scripts, a good chunk of prescription business is repeats, how do we bring in more new customers and turn them into repeats, how do we stop losing repeats.... Price is not the issue as the majority is paid for by the nHS so we get a flat fee for everything to do wiht a prescription and the vast matjority if what goes through is NHS. Care homes business so someone needs to prescribe to all the residents, we have that as well.... So arrest the decline in market share drop, what do we do about store opening hours, how do we optimise what pharmacists, dispensers and dispensing techniciatns do, fully optimise the model in store, how do you boost the number of people coming in with the script in their hands, some of that may be the relationship with GPs within the bounds of the relationship as its allowed..... going through store rationalisation, reduce our cost base without reducing customer base. We are not going to be going on a shopping spree buying up from the independents the pharmacy that is in or next door to the doctors surgery. But one of the things patietns tell us is that convenience is the major factor in choice. Covid work patterns have an impact, it used to be you drop in at boots on the way to work or way home etc. now you just go to the pharmacy by the doctor surgery... On a recruitment drive for pharmacists, so if you walk into a Boots store, teh phrarmacist may or may not be a locum, so we want to reduce the number of locums increase number of perms etc....
JH question - will the combined data let you start to identify the customers who migh want digital savvy click and collect solution with a slot to pick up etc etc. 
yes, Andy talking to MD of pharmcy and its a low bar for using data to run that business, the previous MD was a pharmacist. So new guy Jamie, he is a data driven retailer and he is bringing the retail insight and data to make business decisions so need to sort out some basics like the management information. So the things I suggested are icing and cherry, he says right now he doesnt even have basic cake in terms of analytics, management infromation etc,...... so when you as kthe why quetsions the data isnt there etc etc (JH NOTE - SHARE WHAT WE ARE DOING IN OPTEGRA).... 
Some of the enablers to some of the journey, a thing we started discussing today, at the moment when you are dealing with us as a pharmacy customer, the advantage card isnt really part of the journey. BUT thats how we know who we are dealing with. So the Advantage card becomes the personalisation bit of the experience in pharmacy. But pharmacy has been totally separate for so long its all new...
So we will need sensitive consent separately to marketing consent.... Will have to start with how to rebuild some of our systems to let people capture it, how to rebuild systems to take it into the lake and then what would we execute against it.... So the specific collecting of consent lets you then have personalised very specific journey.... involves email, text, app etc... 
so the other thing is how to manage the data safely, the PII, the consent etc.
But goal, what does a seamless prescription journey look like for different types of customer
Massive growth opportunity and chance to regain lost ground in pharmacy..... 
Grwoth will be the services that are health related that pharmacists are allowed to deliver and at a hihgher margin than prescriptions....
No plans for GP, but people paying to get a mole looked at quick by a pharmacist is very possible. 
And there is Boots online doctor, have an online consultation, Doctors arent bOOts but its our brand,...... Fulfilment rapidly afte rseeing the doctor could be signficiant
But the main event has to be solving the way we run the pharmacy, get back to market share and solve data
interestingly, we dont know what is the reason for the first relationshjip with the pharmacy, we think we know in some cases, student clubs and brands like 17 which brings girls in etc.. Or new mothers with our baby club, but we  dont know waht the entry point is in pharamcy! We know the exit point which is you find a more convenient pharmacist or you pass away or move into home etc.... Hypothesis that diabetes is one of the triggers, but dont know that for certain...
Online and ecommerce space - very much a desire to grow that, its currently probably a 750m turn over business, aspiration is to get to a billion.... then get to 2billion... So how do we provide more delivery options, how do we get quicker website so we lose less people on their journey through browse and shop etc.... And tehre is the marketing o ff the back of that to make it happen, what is our offsite presence, what is working for us etc... No one at the minute is giddy in Boots about metaverse shopping thank goodness...
Increasing focus on genuinely what we mean by omnichannel, thinking abuot browsing store verus shop on line, browse in store versus shop in store etc... 
example- addidas store, once you have bought stuff on oxford street, you can show them your reseat, give them all your shopping INCLUDING the non Addidas stuff yo uhave bought and they will then courier them to your house!!!
JH - do you have formal pilot innovation stores? - not really, we sometimes pick a store and try things but no one innovation store where we always go and trial.... 
Also, some inovations like video watching, ie who puts stuff back after picking it up, they piloted at Milton Keynes, it worked and would apy back but wont deploy as they dont have capital even though there is paybacl.... No real designated sites for pilot....
- Is there a competitor that provides a model of best practice on how data is supporting their growth strategy?
I think everybody would like to say we are as good as Tesco, I do wonder how far off that we really are, I think it depends on different aspects of it... Boots have invested a completely different amount of money in reaching this capability than Tescos (depends if you measure DunnHumbys money as well)... Boots did well on a shoe string...  The big challenge is again the combination of pharmacy and retail data... thats where we are behind.... 
Data questions
- How many years of historical data are in the existing Data Lakehouse, what condition is the data in in terms of consistency, cleanliness etc, what are approximate volumes?
- Which division/department do the analysts belong to and what tools are they using? 
- How is data currently being used for customer engagement? Is there a proactive drive to target consumers with targeted offers and is this store driven or online? Does the basket level data get used to drive shelf space optimisation decisions etc?
- How are you planning to handle GDPR and PII rules around patient data, particularly if you are looking to cross reference patient (pharmacy) and customer (retail) data to the same individual?
- Would there be niche products, such as testing and diagnostics that you would want to target more effectively by analysing the combined patient and customer data (for example, a customer buying a lot of indigestion products might be someone to target for a food allergy test etc etc?) or does this raise more problems than opportunities?
Skills and infrastructure
- Cost optimization for Azure - when did you last have a review of the cloud infrastructure by expert data solution and / or cloud infrastructure architects to review the set up and suggest optimisations and cost down measures? There may be optimisations possible particularly in the storage, utilisation patterns, data efficiency etc.
about a year ago, had a bit of help from Avanard on some of the more architectural stuff, how inefficient some of what we have built with the provocation of you need to simplify some of this, worlds moved on, lets face it you are on a MS treadmill now on development so if you stay on what you were on its goign to get expensive.A lso some ongoing involvement with Dufrain who are looking at some of the deisgn stuff. For Aid there is a cloud centre of excellence and asking them for help and advice on thin ops etc... Are we fully optimised, no, a long way from that, ther e is more broadly an opportunity in that Azure cloud space to see where all the efficiencies are in that cloud stack, part of which is the data platform but of course more and more of hte tech stack is in the Azure cloud now, so definitely on a learning journey in terms of cloud cost control optimisation etc.... SO its interesting but its broader than just data...... 
Entirety of pharm aplatform whic is probably approaching the worlds largest pharmacy platform is all running on the Azure cloud, so significant amounts of data, storage etc, its a bit of a beast, all happened in teh last 4-5 months... 
AId - been separating from Walgreens and as part of that been growign our own capability, but its quite a thin team that is drinking from a hosepipe, so its possible that could be a space to help
- What skillsets are currently deployed offshore, what tasks are they being given to do?
TCS have some good expertise but the struggle is the innovation, probably a team of about 10 internally then rely heavily on TCS....
Across the data bit, probably got 50-60 bodies, largely doing data engineering, building, ingestion, building the code to take data from the file store and the lake and promote it up through various transformations to make it more usable, accessible etc.. They RUN things very well, the ETL etc... But the create and push back on innovation is really not good....
Articulated the possibiluty of us helping create and innovate.... 
For procurement - the standard process is very bureaucratic, BUT we can be pragmatic, if we can find the right answer and the right challenge that isnt being looked at, if it was really innovative we could probably short cut some of the worst of the excess. We do have a one time vendor that is simpler, procurement threshold if we are below that for a life time spend then it can avoid being a 4 plus month..
Would work wtih Steve their regional CIO who is a great guy, very much about execution and speed to value, and Steve is also very aware that the Tata situation needs to mature and not go off the rails, so definite space to play in, find the right niche at the right time...... 
SO we will find somethign that we know would be a basket case for Tata and then we might go and infleunce Steve and then see if we can bring in GT, he also likes disruption and knows it sends a shot across others bows.....
Ade and Andy to go away and think about where there may be some areas we could work.
JOhn to give some thoughts as to where the proofs of concept or areas where we could add value
operational review with real experts (cloud, data etc, top engineers that would cost the earth as consultants or not be available elsewhere)
Build out of some important complex basics, get the management information reporting going etc
Innovation and pilot to get proof of concept into different stores or similar.... combinging data sources, etc etc. 
Send those ideas as food for thought and Aid and Andy will do their own thinking.....', 'JH', '2023-09-04 07:27'),
