  (18406982104::bigint, 7766184420::bigint, null::bigint, 8083576549::bigint, 'BRiefing note between John and Artem:
hi - i put a placeholder for a call in your calendar tomorrow to talk to a medtech company that need some initial architecture work done to test out a concept around developing a platform for medical research. Its at 1pm UK, are you able to join?
Artem Sorokin  8:56 PM

Hey John, yep, I will join
John Harris  9:00 PM

Great, thanks. Company is called EVOTEC, we are talking to the head of their computational drug discovery. He has some ideas about potentially bringing automation to deep search around drug discovery. Heres a few notes:
John Harris  9:06 PM
Evotec challenge - one big thing occupying my mind, that is that the molecular architect is the only place where we have a department where the objectives of medicinal chemistry, computational chemistry and data science are so closely linked. We are going to be offering the modern day way of doing drug discovery, lets just look at the facts, according to the FDA there are 8000 defined human diseases out ther, we have cures for 500 of those... yet modern day drug discovery has been going for over 100 years so we have not got it right... Back in the 90s people said its a paradigm shift and we tried genomics, protonomics etc... but its no one thing, its all of those coming together that we are doing in molecular architecture. BUT its all still very very manual and its folks tinkering around with tech. What would happen if we could get a more focussed open mindset, a biological mindset and expose the tools to everyone....
Go back to early 90s, the internet started to pop up, the techys amongst us said it would never take off, everyoen would need a computer for one thing, that would never take off. At GSK we thought we could expose all of our CompChem tools to everyone in the world, we took all the complexity from all the toolsets and put them on the web. No one used them because we had left the complexity there....
But in the same way that Microsoft office made it possible for us to type our own notes and do our own accounting and slides, can we do the same thing with a simpler usable platform?
What if we did that to drug discovery? So out of the 1.2million articles that are out there around drug discovery, how could we ingest and build knowledge graphs, NLP to build links between phrases. NVIDIA GSK and IBM are all doing it. So then all the knowledge is availabel to our computer.... Lets use GSK as an example, they have built a system that helps them do drug discovery, at the back end is all the tools we have used as as workflow, DMTA Design, Make, Test, Analyse. So to compete we need to compress those DMTA cycles, so we have fewer cycles and make fewer compounds, which lets you go more quickly and more chealpiy (fewer compounds lower cost). And bearing in mind that 99% of discovery work fails....
So the next point is if we think about how big small molecule space is, the small molecule drugs, the things that weigh less than 500 daltons, so small 10 to the 60 (thats a novendisilluum!
). Compare how many molecules do we think pharma has made in the last 100 years......  The reality is that big pharma probably has 1 billion molecules.THat is miniscule, in the number of undiscovered molecules, number would be 10 to the power of 60…. A huge number where 1 billion barely registers.
So how do EVOTEC make this happen? So we have a load of weird science, we have a version of alpha folding we work o nwith proteins etc. And monte carlo tree searches and Basian optimisation... BUT we now need to link this together and automate it, pull these tools together, we are calling this EVOPIPE....  taking nodes and link them together. So its to democratise and save time and money. Starting a new project, all you know on day one is the biological target name. So you go on to google and search that, that gives you the reference data and the tagging, but behind that there is a huge amount of data and nodes that ties the data together. So like company in Wales - Amplify - that do the deep searches and pick up everything that could be deep, then you build Natural Language Processing over it etc. Why couldnt that be automated?
Second thing we look for is do we know what the protein structure looks like, so i go to the PDB and say do I have a sturcture for that, but if I know I am going to do that, why dont I automate it? And if I dont ahve a proteein sturtcure, I can pass it to alpha fold, or to then the other parts of process, etc etc....
And then in my knowledge graph I have everything in one place, that can then drive the biological screening. The idea would be start it on a friday night then by monday morning the knowledge graph is done, searched all the published data pulled out all the knowledge tied into that protein target, all possible today, yet we dont do it todaySo what talent do you need? Its hybrid talent, in the same way you have compchem and trad chem overlap. But I need help, obviously there is architecture around that, there is BI, undersatnding business flow and data, UX is massive,  its putting these highly advanced and complex tools into the hands of people....
UX, UI massively important to me. Its a full systems engineering problem. In a really complex fieldPotential starting point - a really smart architect, a data engineer with sicience background and a UX / UI whiz? Help build business case?
9:10
Sorry, they are pretty long notes, but its one of those long discussions I had with a super smart guy, but he does have a good understanding of what he wants to try and do. Basic assignment will be to spend 3 or 4 days looking at the theory of his idea with current Evotec data and figuring out if his idea is buildable. That would be a funded engagement for us both to either kill the idea or potentially move it to a business case
Artem Sorokin  9:11 PM

Ok, doesn''t it sound like Platinum''s pitch btw?
9:12
Well maybe except data warehouse and search component
John Harris  9:12 PM

Very similar, but more on drug discovery and search than on lab automation
Artem Sorokin  9:12 PM

I mean the pipeline part sounds pretty similar to Platinum
John Harris  9:13 PM

Exactly its the idea of bringing automation to some traditioanally complex manual processes. In this case its about searching for what data already exists to support a piece of research and the theory behind it, today thats a massively manual process. If it could be automated the chance of accelerating basic drug discovery is huge
Artem Sorokin  9:13 PM

Yeah, I can see some vector database as the key component of this
9:14
On the other hand also sounds like it''s domain specific Enterprise search
9:14
Feels like something should already be there applied to drug discovery
9:15
I''d also research existing solutions a bit more
John Harris  9:15 PM

Yeah, he mentioned a company from Wales that I have not looked up yet, called Amplify, they can do some kind of very deep search and he said its that sort of capability that he wants but in a more focussed format
Artem Sorokin  9:17 PM

https://www.addgene.org/vector-database/
John Harris  9:18 PM

Interesting, we should definitely discuss those services with him and see if he is aware or has already given them consideration. He probably is aware as he is super smart, but he is a scientist more than a tech guy although he does understand both worlds
1
Artem Sorokin  9:19 PM

https://milvus.io/docs/v2.0.x/molecular_similarity_search.md

milvus.io
Molecular Similarity Search
Build a molecular similarity search system with Milvus v2.0.x.
1
John Harris  9:26 PM

Cool, those are all really good relevant pieces of thinking, should be a great conversation.', 'JH', '2023-06-26 06:36'),
