  (-386122883263::bigint, 386122883263::bigint, 'Dear Mr Harris,
My name is David Randall, I am a consultant nephrologist based at the Royal London Hospital. Your company was recommended to me by Hakim Yadi who did some consultancy work for our Trust – I gather you have worked with him in the past.

In our hospital we have developed a novel IT tool which we are looking to establish on a more professional basis. The software tool is based around an ETL pipeline working from hospital pathology data. Briefly, it analyses all lab tests performed by our clinical labs, identifies patients meeting diagnostic criteria for different metabolic disorders, integrates this with hospital admission data, outpatient attendance data and primary care data, risk-stratifies patients based on multiple clinical criteria and assesses the degree of current treatment optimisation against national (NICE) guidelines, before generating individual health plans for all patients for implementation by community pharmacists or GPs.

The whole pipeline is currently based around a series of complex SQL Server stored procedures (~25 procedures, each hundreds to a few thousand lines long), run nightly within the hospital data warehouse to populate a series of tables. The system has been active since late July and has already identified >150,000 patients in Northeast London; we project this will rise to ~500,000 or more over coming months. We are already using it to pilot clinical interventions (proactive treatment optimisation clinics), and it is likely to become a major part of Barts Health Trust’s disease prevention programme. We hope in the future to offer/sell it to other NHS organisations if our pilot evaluation suggests it can improve care in line with the ‘three shifts’ of the NHS ‘ten year plan’.

As part of this process, we have secured limited funding to turn our current set of algorithms into a viable, standalone IT product. This would need to:

Meet appropriate quality standards (eg ISO 13485)
Gain MHRA approval as ‘software as a medical device’
Employ robust and resilient system architecture (potentially via a standalone cloud-based server with secure data link to the Trust data warehouse),
Run the algorithms we have developed in-house, developed in collaboration with clinical IT developers within our organisation,
Feature a secure but user-friendly ‘front end’ (probably a WebApp or similar), to allow clinical and administrative users to read and update records within the database tables,
Feature an appropriate data interface with the hospital’s Electronic Patient Record (Oracle Millennium) to allow clinical data to be passed into Millennium for wider visibility,
Feature an appropriate interface (eg using the MESH API or NHS App) to allow clinical documents to be sent to patients and their GPs,
Be deployable to other NHS organisations (by establishing new secure data connections to their clinical databases) to allow future sharing/commercialisation of the product,
Appropriately protect the intellectual property developed within Barts Health NHS Trust.

Much of the backend architecture is currently mature and in regular use: the focus of our requirements is turning these existing ‘in house’ solutions into a product to meet regulatory approvals and (eventually) allow sharing or commercialisation.

I would be grateful for your thoughts on whether this might be something your company might be in a position to assist with, and perhaps for a rough estimate of the costs and timescales involved.

With kind regards,', 'JH', '2025-11-25 04:23'),
